const e={note:"Slavic Christendom received its esoteric literature almost entirely at second hand: the apocrypha, the dreambooks, the demonologies and the astrological handbooks arrived in translation from Greek, chiefly through Bulgaria in the tenth century and again through the second South Slavic wave of the fourteenth, and very little of the corpus was composed from nothing. What makes the tradition extraordinary is what happened afterwards, because Slavonic scribes went on copying, glossing and extending this material for six centuries after Byzantium had ceased to care about much of it, with the result that the Slavonic is now the principal and in some cases the only witness to several Jewish and Greek books that have otherwise vanished. The church's own indices of renounced books are, perversely, the best catalogue we possess of what was actually in circulation: for a number of titles the prohibition is the only evidence the book ever existed at all. And nearly the whole of it survives in late copies, sixteenth to nineteenth century and sometimes later still, of texts demonstrably far older, so that dating any individual charm, dreambook or apocryphon is a matter of argument rather than of reading a colophon; most of this literature is anonymous, and where a name is attached it is usually an attribution rather than an authorship. Church Slavonic is not a dead language but the liturgical tongue of several Orthodox churches at this moment, and the prayers described in these currents belong to a living inheritance as much as to a manuscript record. A word on forms: Old Church Slavonic is given in the standard scholarly transliteration (ě, ǫ, ę, ŭ and ĭ for the jers, š č ž šč), and where a title is cited in its later Church Slavonic or modern Russian shape — as scholarship customarily cites many of these books — the entry says so.",themes:[{slug:"apocryphal-scriptures",title:"Apocryphal Scriptures",native:"отъкръвениѥ",translit:"otŭkrŭvenije",summary:"A translated literature so vast, and so patiently recopied, that a Slavic monastic scriptorium ended up preserving Jewish apocalyptic books which Greek Christendom had let fall.",paragraphs:["The apocryphal corpus in Slavonic is not a fringe of the literature but a substantial fraction of it. From the Preslav and Ohrid schools of the late ninth and tenth centuries onward, translators worked through the Byzantine stock of para-biblical narrative — infancy gospels, apocalypses, testaments, questions-and-answers, lives of Adam — and the results were copied in Bulgaria, Serbia, Rus and later Moldavia and Wallachia without interruption until the age of print and, among the Old Believers, well past it. The manuscripts themselves do not use the word apocrypha. They speak of ложныя книги, false books, and отреченныя книги, renounced ones, and it is worth noticing that the categories are the censors' rather than the copyists': a scribe might complete the index of forbidden titles on one gathering and begin one of the forbidden titles on the next. Almost none of this material is signed, and much of it travels inside larger compendia rather than as separate books. The two great Slavonic containers are the Palaea Historica, a Byzantine retelling of Old Testament history translated from Greek, and the Толковая палея or Palaea Interpretata — the title is cited here in the customary modern Russian form — a Slavonic compilation usually placed in the thirteenth century, in which biblical narrative, apocryphal expansion, exegesis and anti-Jewish polemic are stitched together into a single running commentary.","The most consequential of these survivals is 2 Enoch, cited in the manuscripts under titles of the form Книги святых тайн Еноховых, the Books of the Holy Secrets of Enoch. Until recently it was described as existing in no language but Slavonic; that is no longer quite true, since Coptic fragments of the work were identified among the Qasr Ibrim material in Nubia and announced by Joost Hagen in 2009. The Slavonic remains the whole of the continuous text: something over twenty manuscripts and fragments carry it, dated from the fourteenth century to the eighteenth, in a longer and a shorter recension whose relative priority is still argued. The book takes Enoch up through the heavens, sets him before the face of God, has him written into the celestial scribal office, and returns him to earth for thirty days to instruct his sons before he is taken finally. Its date is one of the more strenuous disputes in the study of the pseudepigrapha. Józef Milik pushed it down to the ninth century of our era, essentially treating it as a late Christian composition; John Collins and others, reasoning from its interest in sacrifice and its silence about the destruction of the Temple, place the original no later than the first century and squarely within Second Temple Judaism. Francis Andersen's translation in Charlesworth's Old Testament Pseudepigrapha (1983) and Grant Macaskill's edition of the Slavonic texts (2013) are the working tools; neither settles the question.","The Apocalypse of Abraham is the second such case. It survives in Slavonic alone, and its oldest and only textually independent witness is the Codex Sylvester, a Novgorodian parchment miscellany of the second half of the fourteenth century now in the Russian State Archive of Ancient Acts (fond 381, no. 53); the foliation of the work within the codex is variously cited, and is not repeated here. Every other copy has been absorbed into the Palaea Interpretata. The first part retells Abraham's discovery that his father's idols are wood; the second is a full-dress apocalypse, in which the angel Yahoel conducts Abraham upward, the fallen Azazel contests his ascent and is rebuked, and he is shown the whole course of history in a single picture. Scholars generally place the Greek original, itself rendering a Semitic text, in the decades after 70. The Ladder of Jacob, shorter and likewise preserved only in Slavonic and likewise embedded in the Palaea, expands the dream at Bethel into an angelic interpretation of the ladder's rungs. Alexander Kulik's retroversion of the Apocalypse (2004) and the survey by Kulik and Sergey Minov, Biblical Pseudepigrapha in Slavonic Tradition (2016), set out both what can be recovered and what cannot.","Alongside these stand works whose Greek originals do survive but whose Slavonic career was longer and more vigorous. The Descent of the Virgin into Hell, Хождение Богородицы по мукам, renders the Greek Apocalypse of the Theotokos: the Mother of God is taken by the archangel Michael through the places of punishment, sees who is there and why, and refuses to leave off pleading until a respite is granted the damned. Its Slavonic copies begin in the twelfth century, which makes it one of the earliest surviving witnesses to the whole apocryphal corpus in the language, and its hold on the imagination was long — it is the poem Ivan Karamazov recalls before telling the legend of the Grand Inquisitor. The Gospel of Nicodemus, with its Acts of Pilate and its account of the harrowing of hell, supplied the East Slavic and Balkan visual tradition with much of what it needed for the Anastasis; the iconography and the apocryphon travelled together.","The point worth pressing is the mechanism. A body of Jewish apocalyptic literature, composed in Hebrew or Aramaic, translated into Greek, and then abandoned by the Greek church, was carried across into a young Slavonic Christian literature that had no humanist filter, no critical scholarship, no reason to prune, and a monastic copying culture with immense stamina. What Byzantium dropped, Novgorod and Tarnovo and the Athonite Slav houses kept. That is a genuine and unusual debt. It carries an equally genuine caution: a text that exists only in Slavonic exists only at the end of a chain we cannot check at any point along its length, and every reconstruction of what stood in the lost Greek, still more of what stood in the lost Semitic, is an argument rather than a reading. The Slavonic scribes were faithful, but they were also glossators, harmonisers and abbreviators, and the retroversion of a Slavonic apocryphon is a technique with a literature of its own."],terms:[{native:"отъкръвениѥ",translit:"otŭkrŭvenije",gloss:"uncovering, revelation; the Old Church Slavonic calque of Greek ἀποκάλυψις, built on the verb otŭkryti, to uncover"},{native:"Енохъ",translit:"Enochŭ",gloss:"Enoch; the seventh from Adam, scribe of the heavenly court, and the figure to whom the book preserved in Slavonic is attributed — an attribution, not an authorship"},{native:"палеꙗ",translit:"paleja",gloss:"Palaea, from Greek παλαιά (διαθήκη), the Old Covenant; the name of two distinct compendia of Old Testament narrative and comment. The iotified a ꙗ is the OCS letter"},{native:"Толковая палея",translit:"Tolkovaja paleja",gloss:"the Explanatory Palaea, a Slavonic compilation in which most Slavonic copies of the Apocalypse of Abraham and the Ladder of Jacob are embedded; cited here, as scholarship cites it, in the modern Russian form"},{native:"мѫка",translit:"mǫka",gloss:"torment; the plural stands in the title of the Descent of the Virgin, her walk 'through the torments'. The big yus ѫ is the OCS nasal o"},{native:"лѣствица",translit:"lěstvica",gloss:"ladder; the title-word of the Ladder of Jacob, and the same word that names John Climacus's Ladder of Divine Ascent in Slavonic"},{native:"ложныя книги",translit:"ložnyja knigi",gloss:"false books; one of the two standing manuscript names for the apocrypha, the other being otrečennyja knigi, renounced books. Both are later Church Slavonic forms"}],texts:[{title:"2 Enoch (the Book of the Secrets of Enoch), Slavonic and, since 2009, Coptic",note:"Over twenty Slavonic manuscripts and fragments, fourteenth to eighteenth century, in a long and a short recension; Coptic fragments from Qasr Ibrim identified by Joost Hagen in 2009. F. I. Andersen's translation in James Charlesworth, ed., The Old Testament Pseudepigrapha, vol. 1 (1983); Grant Macaskill, The Slavonic Texts of 2 Enoch (Brill, 2013). The date of the original is disputed between the first century and the ninth."},{title:"The Apocalypse of Abraham, Slavonic only",note:"Oldest witness the Codex Sylvester, RGADA f. 381 no. 53, second half of the fourteenth century, Novgorod; all other copies sit inside the Palaea Interpretata. Edition by Ryszard Rubinkiewicz; retroversion and commentary by Alexander Kulik, Retroverting Slavonic Pseudepigrapha (2004)."},{title:"The Ladder of Jacob, Slavonic only",note:"An expansion of Genesis 28 with an angelic interpretation of the ladder; transmitted within the Palaea Interpretata. Translated by H. G. Lunt in Charlesworth, ed., The Old Testament Pseudepigrapha, vol. 2 (1985)."},{title:"The Descent of the Virgin into Hell (Хождение Богородицы по мукам)",note:"The Slavonic Apocalypse of the Theotokos, from a Greek original whose core is usually placed in the fifth century or later. Slavonic copies from the twelfth century onward, making it among the earliest surviving apocrypha in the language."},{title:"The Gospel of Nicodemus / Acts of Pilate, in Slavonic",note:"The trial before Pilate and the harrowing of hell; the principal literary source behind the Slavonic and Byzantine iconography of the Anastasis."},{title:"Alexander Kulik and Sergey Minov, Biblical Pseudepigrapha in Slavonic Tradition (Oxford, 2016)",note:"The current point of entry to the whole corpus: texts, translations, manuscript histories and a sober account of what retroversion can and cannot establish."}]},{slug:"solomonic-magical-texts",title:"Solomonic Magical Texts",native:"Соломонъ",translit:"Solomonŭ",summary:"The king who bound demons to build the Temple, and the centaur he outwitted — a Solomonic tradition that in Slavonic turns narrative rather than operative.",paragraphs:["Solomon arrives in Slavonic literature already an exorcist. The seed is scriptural — the wisdom that spoke of the trees and the beasts in 1 Kings, the knowledge of the powers of spirits and the virtues of roots in the Wisdom of Solomon — and it was fully grown by the first century, when Josephus described a Jewish exorcist named Eleazar drawing a demon out through a man's nostrils by means of a ring set with a root that Solomon had prescribed. The Greek Testament of Solomon gathers this into a book: the archangel Michael sends Solomon a signet, the demon Ornias is stamped with it, Beelzeboul is stamped in turn, and the whole hierarchy of spirits is set to work on the Temple, each one interrogated as to its name, its star, its particular mischief and the angel who thwarts it. What crossed into Slavonic was not that book entire but its furniture: the ring, the seal, the compelled demons, the Temple raised by unwilling hands, and above all the principle that a demon named is a demon governed.","The distinctively Slavic development is Kitovras. The name is a deformation of κένταυρος, and the story is a recognisable cousin of the tale of Solomon and Ashmedai in the Babylonian Talmud, Gittin 68a–b. Solomon needs the shamir, the stone or worm that cuts stone without iron, because iron may not be lifted over the Temple. He sends for Kitovras, whose well is found and whose wine is drugged, and who is taken in chains. On the road the captive behaves oddly, in a series of episodes shared with the Talmudic tale: he swerves aside from a wedding party, weeps at a merchant selling shoes to a man with days to live, laughs at a diviner who cannot see the treasure beneath his own feet. Brought to Jerusalem, he yields the shamir, and then, in the sequel, tricks Solomon into loosing him and hurls the king to the ends of the earth. In the Palaea Interpretata the cycle sits among the Суды or Judgements of Solomon, one of which is the biblical dispute over the child and the rest of which are not. A fifteenth-century Russian scribe of the Kirillo-Belozersk house, Efrosin, rewrote the material freely in his own miscellany, which is how we know the tradition was still generative and not merely copied — and he is one of the few named hands in this whole hall.","The church was clear about it. Fourteenth-century South Slavic and Russian indices of renounced books list, in a formula that recurs with small variations, the fables and profanities concerning Solomon the king and Kitovras; among the early copies customarily cited is a Nomocanon of the fourteenth century in M. P. Pogodin's collection, though the recensional history of these lists makes any claim to the earliest occurrence provisional. The prohibition failed comprehensively. The tales went on being copied, they migrated into the Palaea, which was itself a respectable book, and they left their traces in Russian folk narrative and eventually in the printed lubok. The relation between the western Solomon-and-Marcolf cycle and the Slavic Solomon-and-Kitovras cycle was the subject of Aleksandr Veselovsky's Slavic Legends of Solomon and Kitovras and the Western Legends of Morolf and Merlin (1872), which remains the point of departure, and the source-critical questions about how the Palaea assembled its Solomon material are still open.","As for the seals and the ring in actual use: what the Slavonic record shows is inherited Byzantine amulet practice rather than a native grimoire tradition. The pentalpha and the hexagram appear as apotropaic marks; the holy rider spearing a prostrate demon, a Byzantine and late-antique amulet type sometimes labelled with Solomon's name, is known in Rus; and Solomon's name occurs in later manuscript charms among the other names of power. What is absent is telling. There is no medieval Slavonic Clavicula Salomonis, no Slavonic Almadel, no vernacular book of conjurations to match the Latin ones. The operative grimoire arrives in Russia late — in the seventeenth and eighteenth centuries, through Polish and German intermediaries — and W. F. Ryan's Bathhouse at Midnight is the survey that establishes this and dates it. Before that, Slavonic Solomon is a figure of story and of prayer, not of the circle and the triangle."],terms:[{native:"Соломонъ",translit:"Solomonŭ",gloss:"Solomon; in Slavonic tradition the wise king, the builder of the Temple, and the master of spirits, but rarely the author of a working book of magic"},{native:"Китоврасъ",translit:"Kitovrasŭ",gloss:"Kitovras, the centaur-demon; the name is a Slavonic deformation of Greek κένταυρος, the role a reworking of the Talmudic Ashmedai"},{native:"печать",translit:"pečatĭ",gloss:"seal, signet; the instrument by which the demon is stamped and bound, and by extension the mark set at the close of a charm"},{native:"прьстень",translit:"prĭstenĭ",gloss:"ring; the Old Church Slavonic form, in which the syllabic r is written ⟨рь⟩, the letter r followed by the front jer ь. East Slavic manuscripts write perstenĭ"},{native:"бѣсъ",translit:"běsŭ",gloss:"demon, evil spirit; the standard OCS rendering of δαίμων and of πνεῦμα ἀκάθαρτον, and the word around which the whole Slavonic demonology is built"},{native:"кощюна",translit:"koščjuna",gloss:"a profane or heathen tale; the word the indices use for the Solomon and Kitovras stories, paired with basni, fables"},{native:"храмъ",translit:"chramŭ",gloss:"temple, house; the building the demons are set to raise, and in Christian Slavonic usage also the church itself"}],texts:[{title:"The Solomon and Kitovras cycle in the Palaea Interpretata",note:"Anonymous. The tale of the shamir, the capture at the well, the three roadside portents, and Kitovras's escape; transmitted among the 'Judgements of Solomon', only one of which is biblical."},{title:"The tale of Solomon and Kitovras in Efrosin's miscellany, Kirillo-Belozersk, later fifteenth century",note:"A free Russian reworking by a named scribe — a rarity in this literature — and evidence that the material was still being composed with rather than merely copied."},{title:"The Testament of Solomon (Greek)",note:"The fullest ancient statement of Solomon as binder of demons: the ring from Michael, the interrogation of the spirits by name and star, the Temple built by compulsion. Dates proposed range from the first century to the high Middle Ages; the Slavonic tradition took its motifs rather than the book."},{title:"The indices of renounced books, entry on Solomon and Kitovras",note:"'Fables and profanities of Solomon the king and of Kitovras' appears in South Slavic and Russian lists from the fourteenth century, a Nomocanon in M. P. Pogodin's collection among those customarily cited."},{title:"A. N. Veselovsky, Славянские сказания о Соломоне и Китоврасе и западные легенды о Морольфе и Мерлине (St Petersburg, 1872)",note:"The comparative study that placed the Slavic cycle beside Solomon and Marcolf; superseded in detail, still the frame within which the question is posed."},{title:"W. F. Ryan, The Bathhouse at Midnight: An Historical Survey of Magic and Divination in Russia (Stroud and University Park, 1999)",note:"Establishes, among much else, that the operative Solomonic grimoire reaches Russia only in the seventeenth and eighteenth centuries and by western routes."}]},{slug:"protective-prayers-amulets",title:"Protective Prayers and Amulets",native:"змеевик",translit:"zmeevik",summary:"Gold medallions with an archangel on one face and a nest of serpents on the other, birch-bark charms scratched by laymen, and a prayer book that could carry both kinds of text without embarrassment.",paragraphs:["The objects modern scholarship calls змеевики — the word is a modern Russian scholarly label, not a medieval one — are pendant medallions, cast or struck in gold, silver, bronze or carved in stone, bearing a Christian image on one face and on the other a human head from which serpents radiate. They were made and worn in Rus from about the eleventh century to the sixteenth, and their catalogue is Tatiana Nikolaeva and Aleksei Chernetsov's Древнерусские амулеты-змеевики (Moscow, 1991). The most famous is the so-called Chernigov hryvnia, a heavy gold medallion found by accident in 1821 on the bank of the Bilous near Chernihiv — the published measurements vary between accounts and are not repeated here: the archangel Michael on one side with the trisagion in Greek, the serpent-head on the other with a Slavonic petition asking the Lord to help his servant Vasilii. Since Vasilii was the baptismal name of Vladimir Monomakh, who ruled at Chernigov, the piece has been attributed to him since Metropolitan Evgenii Bolkhovitinov first studied it — an inference rather than a documented fact, and worth flagging as such. The serpent-head itself is Byzantine, descended from the ὑστέρα amulets against the wandering womb, and the class was worn by bishops as freely as by princes.","The second great protective formula is the Sisinnius prayer. Behind it lies one of the oldest continuously attested magical narratives in the eastern Mediterranean, the story of the child-stealing female demon confronted by a holy man who compels her to recite her names; the same historiola is found in Aramaic, Greek, Coptic, Ethiopic, Armenian and Romanian, and the Slavonic is one branch of it. In the Slavonic rendering the demon has become plural. The fevers are sisters, usually said to be daughters of Herod, who rise from a lake or from the sea; Saint Sisinnius, sometimes with a companion named Sikhail, meets them, demands who they are and where they are going, and hears them name themselves one by one — names that describe what each does to the body, the shaker, the burner, the yellower, the deafener, the one that will not let a person eat. Their number is unstable in a way that is itself informative: seven, nine, twelve, forty, seventy-seven, the last of these being the round figure of indefinite multitude that recurs throughout the Slavic charm tradition, in which afflictions are driven off seventy-seven versts or dispersed into seventy-seven forms. The texts are described here as a genre; none is given as a formula to be used.","That this was not a clerical fantasy but a practice of ordinary literate townspeople is demonstrated, unusually and decisively, by the birch bark of Novgorod. Document 734, stratigraphically placed in the middle decades of the twelfth century, is a charm in which the name Sikhail and the word angel are each repeated three times. Document 715, from the first half of the thirteenth century, invokes thrice-nine angels and thrice-nine archangels to deliver a man named Mikhei from his sickness, by the prayers of the Mother of God. Document 930, a fragment from the turn of the fourteenth and fifteenth centuries excavated at the Nikitinsky site, is the Sisinnius legend itself: the saint and Sikhail on Mount Sinai, and the women rising from the water. These are the editions of A. A. Zaliznyak and his colleagues, and they matter because they are dated by stratigraphy rather than by palaeography — a fixed point in a field where almost everything else floats.","What the birch bark also shows is that the line between prayer and charm was not where a modern reader would draw it, and possibly was not drawn at all. The oldest Slavonic prayer book, the Glagolitic Euchologium Sinaiticum of the eleventh century from St Catherine's on Sinai, already contains among its offices exorcistic prayers attributed to Basil the Great, and those prayers remained in the Требникъ, the book of occasional offices, thereafter. A Trebnik of the sixteenth or seventeenth century may hold the canonical office for the blessing of water, the prayers over a sick man, the prayers against the worm in cattle, and — copied in the same hand, on the same paper, with the same red initials — a charm that no council ever authorised. Peter Mohyla's great Kievan Euchologion of 1646 is in part an attempt to impose order on exactly this. The continuum was not a scandal to the men who wrote these books; it became one only when someone was asked to classify it. The Trebnik itself is in use in Orthodox churches today, which is a reason to speak of these prayers in the present tense and with care."],terms:[{native:"змеевик",translit:"zmeevik",gloss:"'serpent-thing'; the modern Russian scholarly name for the amulet type with a Christian image on one face and a serpent-haired head on the other. The medieval owners had no such word for it"},{native:"Черниговская гривна",translit:"Černigovskaja grivna",gloss:"the Chernigov hryvnia, a gold zmeevik found in 1821 near Chernihiv, traditionally though not certainly associated with Vladimir Monomakh, baptised Vasilii; the name is the modern Russian one"},{native:"Сисиний",translit:"Sisinij",gloss:"Sisinnius, the saint who confronts the fever-demons and forces them to name themselves; given in the modern Russian form of the name. The Slavonic branch of a historiola attested across the eastern Mediterranean"},{native:"Сихаилъ",translit:"Sichailŭ",gloss:"Sikhail, the angel written three times on Novgorod birch bark 734 and named beside Sisinnius on birch bark 930; not one of the archangels the church names"},{native:"ⰿⱁⰾⰻⱅⰲⰰ",translit:"molitva",gloss:"prayer, in Glagolitic — the alphabet of the Euchologium Sinaiticum, the oldest surviving Slavonic prayer book, which already carries exorcistic prayers under the name of Basil the Great"},{native:"Требникъ",translit:"Trebnikŭ",gloss:"the book of occasional offices — baptisms, funerals, blessings, prayers over the sick; the volume in which authorised prayer and unauthorised charm most often ended up sharing a quire, and a book still in liturgical use"},{native:"берестяная грамота",translit:"berestjanaja gramota",gloss:"birch-bark document, in the modern Russian term; the Novgorod finds, dated by stratigraphy, give the charm tradition its only securely dated early witnesses"},{native:"оберегъ",translit:"oberegŭ",gloss:"a protective object or formula, from oberegati, to guard; a later Russian term applied retrospectively to the whole apotropaic class"}],texts:[{title:"Novgorod birch bark 734, mid-twelfth century",note:"A charm: the name Sikhail three times, the word angel three times. Stratigraphically dated, and thus among the earliest datable Slavonic charms in existence."},{title:"Novgorod birch bark 715, first half of the thirteenth century",note:"A charm-prayer against fever invoking thrice-nine angels and thrice-nine archangels for the servant Mikhei, by the prayers of the Theotokos."},{title:"Novgorod birch bark 930, turn of the fourteenth and fifteenth centuries",note:"A fragment of the Sisinnius legend against fever, with Sisinnius and Sikhail on Mount Sinai; from the Nikitinsky excavation."},{title:"Euchologium Sinaiticum, eleventh century, Glagolitic (St Catherine's, Sinai)",note:"The oldest Slavonic prayer book: liturgical offices, a penitential, and exorcistic prayers under the name of Basil the Great, which pass from here into the later Trebnik."},{title:"T. V. Nikolaeva and A. V. Chernetsov, Древнерусские амулеты-змеевики (Moscow: Nauka, 1991)",note:"The catalogue and analysis of the known corpus of zmeeviki, eleventh to sixteenth century, with the argument about what the serpent composition meant to the people who wore it."},{title:"Peter Mohyla, Euchologion or Trebnik (Kiev, 1646)",note:"A monumental attempt to fix the content of the occasional offices for the Kievan metropolitanate, and by implication to decide what did not belong in them."}]},{slug:"angelology-demonology",title:"Angelology and Demonology",native:"ангелъ",translit:"angelŭ",summary:"Nine ranks translated from the Areopagite, seven archangels the church would name, a great many angels it would not, and the earliest recorded use of the word vampire sitting in a scribe's signature.",paragraphs:["The learned angelology reached the Slavs by two routes. The earlier ran through John Damascene: at Preslav, John the Exarch of Bulgaria translated a substantial part of the Exposition of the Orthodox Faith under the title Небеса, the Heavens, and with it came the Damascene's summary of the celestial orders. The fuller route opened much later, in 1371, when Isaija of Serres completed a Serbian Church Slavonic translation of the whole Corpus of Dionysius the Areopagite — the Celestial Hierarchy, the Ecclesiastical Hierarchy, the Divine Names, the Mystical Theology, the letters and the scholia. The colophon Isaija attached to it is one of the most quoted passages in Serbian literature, a lament for a country ruined at the Marica in the same year, begun, as he says, in happier times. From that translation the nine ranks in their three triads entered Slavonic as fixed vocabulary: seraphim, cherubim, thrones; dominions, virtues, powers; principalities, archangels, angels. Their status was never in doubt, but it should be said that the Areopagitic corpus is a philosophical theology of mediated illumination, not an operative angel-magic, and Slavonic readers took it as the former.","Below the ranks the tradition names individuals. Michael and Gabriel are scriptural; Raphael comes with Tobit; Uriel with 4 Ezra; and to these the Russian iconographic tradition added Selaphiel, Jegudiel and Barachiel, sometimes an eighth in Jeremiel, to make the assembly commemorated at the Synaxis of the Archangel Michael on the eighth of November. Every baptised person had a guardian angel and, in the East Slavic reckoning, a name-day saint who functioned similarly. Against this orderly list one may set the angels of the charms, who are numerous, often unattested elsewhere, and occasionally suspicious. Sikhail on the Novgorod birch bark is the clearest case: an angel with a perfectly regular theophoric ending, a firm place beside Sisinnius in the fever narrative, and no place at all in any calendar. Church legislation against invoking angels by unknown names is old and Greek, and its Slavonic afterlife is a good index of what people were actually doing.","The demonology has three layers that never quite fused. The first is patristic: the demon is a fallen angel, incorporeal, malicious and defeated, and the word for him is бѣсъ. The second is inherited from the pre-Christian Slavic dead. The Primary Chronicle records under the year 6600, that is 1092, that at Polotsk something moved through the streets at night, that anyone who went out to look was wounded invisibly and died, that the thing then began to appear by day on horseback leaving hoofprints, and that people said the навье were striking the men of Polotsk — навье being the dead, and specifically the dead who have not been settled. The eleventh- to thirteenth-century anti-pagan homily known as the Word of Saint Gregory, itself an adaptation of a Greek original and a hostile witness, complains that the Slavs had once offered to the упыри and the берегыни before they offered to Perun. The third layer is the fever-sisters, the трясавицы, daughters of Herod, whose names are their symptoms and whose number is anything from seven to seventy-seven; they are demons in the sense that they are personal, nameable and expellable, but they belong to the charm rather than to the dogmatic tradition.","The word упырь has a remarkable first appearance. In 1047 a Novgorod priest copied out the books of the prophets with commentary for Prince Vladimir Yaroslavich, working, as his note says, from the fourteenth of May to the nineteenth of December, and signed his work: попъ Оупирь Лихыи, the priest Upir Likhyi, which is to say Wicked Vampire. This is the earliest recorded use of the word known in any language. What it means is contested. It may be an ordinary pre-Christian nickname surviving as a personal name, which is the usual view; Anders Sjöberg suggested in 1982 that it renders the Norse name Öpir, which would deflate the story considerably. The colophon also says he copied ис коуриловицѣ, from the kurilovica, and whether that names the Glagolitic alphabet of Constantine-Cyril or the Cyrillic that bears his name has been argued for a century. The 1047 autograph is lost; the colophon survives only because a small number of fifteenth- and sixteenth-century copyists reproduced it along with the text — the hall's whole methodological problem in a single line.","Two smaller matters deserve notice. Later Russian Church Slavonic orthography developed a convention of writing the Greek loanword for angel in two ways: contracted under a titlo when a holy angel is meant, and spelled out as аггелъ, with the double gamma transliterated, when a fallen one is. The same word, the same etymology, the same being before the fall, distinguished on the page by a scribal decision. And the Bogomils, treated at length in their own current, are reported to have taken the patristic account of the fall and made a cosmogony of it: in the doctrine their opponents ascribe to them, Satanail is not an angel who rebelled within a world God had already made, but the elder son who made the visible world himself, so that matter is his residue rather than God's handiwork. Everything the Orthodox tradition says about demons assumes they are trespassers in someone else's creation. The dualist claim, as we have it, is that they are at home."],terms:[{native:"ангелъ",translit:"angelŭ",gloss:"angel, from Greek ἄγγελος; in liturgical books written contracted under a titlo when a holy angel is meant"},{native:"аггелъ",translit:"aggelŭ",gloss:"the same word spelled out with the Greek double gamma; in later Russian Church Slavonic convention reserved for a fallen angel, so that orthography does the work of theology"},{native:"чинъ",translit:"činŭ",gloss:"rank, order; the term used for each of the nine Dionysian hierarchies, and also for the order of a liturgical service"},{native:"трясавица",translit:"trjasavica",gloss:"fever-demon, 'the shaker'; the sisters called daughters of Herod, numbered variously at seven, nine, twelve, forty or seventy-seven. The form is later Church Slavonic"},{native:"навье",translit:"navĭe",gloss:"the unquiet dead; the word in the Primary Chronicle's entry for 1092, where the people of Polotsk say the navĭe are striking them"},{native:"оупирь",translit:"upirĭ",gloss:"revenant, vampire; the spelling with the digraph ou, as in the 1047 colophon, which copies also give as upyrĭ. Its appearance there is the earliest recorded use of the word known in any language"},{native:"берегыня",translit:"beregynja",gloss:"a female spirit named beside the upiri in the anti-pagan Word of Saint Gregory; the etymology and even the reading have been argued over"},{native:"Михаилъ",translit:"Michailŭ",gloss:"Michael, the archangel of the Synaxis of 8 November and the commonest figure on the Christian face of the zmeevik amulets"}],texts:[{title:"Dionysius the Areopagite, Corpus Areopagiticum, Slavonic translation by Isaija of Serres, completed 1371",note:"The first complete Slavonic Dionysius, in the Serbian recension, with the translator's celebrated colophon lamenting the catastrophe at the Marica in the year he finished."},{title:"John the Exarch of Bulgaria, Небеса",note:"A Preslav-school translation of much of John Damascene's Exposition of the Orthodox Faith, and the earlier channel by which the doctrine of the angelic orders reached Slavonic."},{title:"The colophon of Upir Likhyi, 1047",note:"Appended to a copy of the prophets with commentary made for Prince Vladimir Yaroslavich of Novgorod between 14 May and 19 December. The autograph is lost; the note survives only in later copies of the fifteenth and sixteenth centuries."},{title:"The Primary Chronicle (Повесть временных лет), entry for 6600 / 1092",note:"The Polotsk episode: invisible riders, hoofprints, and the report that the navĭe are striking the townspeople. Later readers have proposed an epidemic; the chronicler proposes the dead."},{title:"The Word of Saint Gregory (Слово святого Григория), eleventh to thirteenth century",note:"An anonymous anti-pagan homily adapted from a Greek original, which lists offerings made to the upiri and the beregyni as older than the cult of Perun. A hostile witness, and the usual caveats apply."},{title:"A. A. Zaliznyak, Древненовгородский диалект, 2nd edn (Moscow, 2004)",note:"The linguistic and editorial foundation for the Novgorod birch-bark corpus, including the charm texts, and the reason those texts can be dated at all."}]},{slug:"exorcisms-healing-charms",title:"Exorcisms and Healing Charms",native:"заговоръ",translit:"zagovorŭ",summary:"A genre with an architecture: the setting out, the sacred geography, the encounter, the command, and the lock — described here as historians describe it, and not set down to be used.",paragraphs:["The East Slavic заговор is one of the most tightly patterned oral-literary genres in Europe, and the pattern is what makes it recognisable across four centuries of manuscripts and a century and a half of field collection. Scholars describe it as opening with a formula of setting out: the speaker rises, blesses himself, crosses himself, goes out of the door and out of the gate, and the movement is described in a widening series that carries him from the domestic interior into the open field. There follows a sacred topography that is nowhere on any map — the open field, the ocean-sea, the island of Buyan standing in it, and on the island the white stone Alatyr, beneath or upon which sits the figure who is addressed. There is an encounter: Christ, the Mother of God, a named saint, three sisters, three brothers, a rider, an old man. There is a command, sometimes framed as a request and sometimes not, in which the ailment is ordered out and away. And there is a closing, the замок or lock, in which the words are sealed and the key thrown into the sea, or the speaker declares that his word is firm and his lock is strong. Some texts leave out one element. Very few leave out three.","The engine inside this frame is the historiola: a short narrative of something that once happened, whose recital was itself held to be the operation. Sisinnius met the fevers and made them name themselves; therefore the fevers were named and went. Christ met a man on the road and healed him; therefore. The Mother of God wept and the bleeding stopped; therefore. The logic is precedent, not petition, and it is very old — it is the logic of the Egyptian and Babylonian incantation and of the Greek magical papyri, and it survives into Slavonic Christianity without any perceptible break, which is one of the reasons the genre is so hard to date. A charm written down in a Vologda notebook in the eighteenth century may preserve a structure a millennium older, and there is usually no internal evidence to prove or disprove it. The manuscripts themselves are mostly late and almost wholly anonymous: the great collections of Russian charms from handwritten sources run from the seventeenth century into the nineteenth.","A second recurring mechanism is transfer. The illness is not destroyed but moved: onto a stone, into a tree — the aspen has a particular reputation — into running water, into the sea, onto a dog or a bird, into a wall, into the empty places where no cock crows, where no plough goes, where no smoke rises. Ethnographers and court records report that the physical acts accompanying the words followed the same grammar. Something was measured with thread and the thread disposed of; a sick child was passed through a cleft in a tree or a hole in a wall and the gap closed behind; water was spoken over and given to drink; the practitioner breathed or spat three times over the shoulder. These are recorded observations, not directions. Anthropologically they are transactions with a place, and the place is nearly always liminal — the threshold, the crossroads, the riverbank, the bathhouse at midnight, from which W. F. Ryan took the title of the standard survey.","The awkward and well-documented fact about all of this is that the clergy were on both sides of it. The Stoglav council of 1551 legislated against sorcerers and diviners and against the renounced books; ecclesiastical courts and, from the seventeenth century, secular ones prosecuted people for possessing written charms, sometimes to death, and Valerie Kivelson's Desperate Magic (2013) shows in detail how such cases ran and whom they caught, which was disproportionately men and disproportionately the unfree. And yet a very large proportion of the surviving manuscript charms are in clerical or semi-clerical hands, bound with prayers, written in the same book as the psalter, using the same red ink for the same kind of initial. Priests condemned them from the ambo and copied them in the evening. Everything here is offered as historical description: no charm is reproduced as an operative formula, and nothing that accompanied the words in practice is set down as instruction."],terms:[{native:"заговоръ",translit:"zagovorŭ",gloss:"the charm or incantation as a genre; from govoriti, to speak. Also called a nagovor, prigovor or slovo depending on region and period"},{native:"Алатырь",translit:"Alatyrĭ",gloss:"the white stone at the centre of the charm cosmos, also Latyrĭ; its name has been derived from alabaster, from amber and from the altar, and none of the derivations has carried the field"},{native:"Буянъ",translit:"Bujanŭ",gloss:"the island in the ocean-sea on which the stone stands; the fixed geography of the charms, which corresponds to no actual place"},{native:"окиянъ",translit:"okijanŭ",gloss:"the ocean-sea, from Greek ὠκεανός; almost always paired with more, sea, in the formula more-okijan"},{native:"влъхвъ",translit:"vlŭchvŭ",gloss:"magus, sorcerer; the OCS form, used in the Gospel for the magi from the east and in the chronicles for the pagan diviners who led risings in eleventh-century Rus. East Slavic writes volchvŭ"},{native:"чародѣи",translit:"čaroděi",gloss:"charm-worker, from čara, a spell or enchantment; one of the standing terms in the conciliar legislation against magic"},{native:"зелиѥ",translit:"zelije",gloss:"herbs, potion; the root of Zelejnikŭ, the herbal that appears on the indices of renounced books"},{native:"замокъ",translit:"zamokŭ",gloss:"lock; the closing formula that seals the charm, often paired with a key thrown into the sea or with the declaration that the speaker's word is firm"}],texts:[{title:"Русские заговоры из рукописных источников XVII – первой половины XIX в., ed. A. L. Toporkov (Moscow, 2010)",note:"The principal modern edition of Russian charms from manuscript rather than from oral collection, with the codicological detail that shows in whose books they were written. The texts themselves are anonymous."},{title:"W. F. Ryan, The Bathhouse at Midnight (1999)",note:"The standard survey in English of Russian magic and divination from the earliest evidence to the eighteenth century, and the fullest treatment of the charm's structure and its Byzantine and folk components."},{title:"Valerie A. Kivelson, Desperate Magic: The Moral Economy of Witchcraft in Seventeenth-Century Russia (Cornell, 2013)",note:"Court records rather than charm texts: who was prosecuted for magic in Muscovy, on what evidence, and with what outcome. Corrects several long-standing assumptions, including about gender."},{title:"The Stoglav (Стоглавъ), council of 1551",note:"The hundred chapters, which legislate against sorcerers, diviners and the renounced books, and in doing so supply a list of what was current."},{title:"The Sisinnius prayer against the fevers, Slavonic recensions",note:"Anonymous and preserved in many late copies; the most widely transmitted single healing text in the tradition, moving between the prayer book, the amulet and the oral charm."},{title:"Incantatio: An International Journal on Charms, Charmers and Charming (from 2011)",note:"The journal of the ISFNR Committee on Charms, Charmers and Charming, and the current venue for comparative work on the Slavic material alongside the wider European corpus."}]},{slug:"dream-books-divination",title:"Dream Books and Divination",native:"Сносудецъ",translit:"Snosudecŭ",summary:"Thunder read by the month, the future read from a twitching eyelid, the year read from the weekday of Christmas — and every one of these books condemned by an index that the same scribes were copying.",paragraphs:["The Slavonic divinatory handbooks are almost all translations, almost all from Greek, and almost all anonymous. The dreambook is the oldest of them in Slavonic. The indices name it Сносудецъ, a precise calque of the Greek ὀνειροκριτικόν — dream-judger — and the same object appears in the manuscripts as Сновидец and, later and most commonly, Сонникъ. Copies attribute it to the prophet Daniel, for the obvious reason that Daniel interprets dreams in scripture; the attribution is a convention of the genre rather than a claim anyone could test, and it links the Slavonic material to the enormous Latin Somniale Danielis family and behind both to the Oneirocritica of Artemidorus and to the Byzantine dreambooks that go under the name of Achmet. The form is a simple alphabetical or thematic list: to see this in a dream signifies that. It is not a psychology and does not pretend to be one; it is a lookup table, and it was used as one.","The Громникъ prognosticates from thunder — specifically from the month, and in the fuller redactions from the zodiacal sign the sun stands in, when thunder is first heard. It is known in several distinct Slavonic redactions, all of them descending from a Greek βροντολόγιον; the translation is South Slavic and no later than the fourteenth century, and Russian copies run from the fifteenth century to the eighteenth. Its predictions are agrarian and political in about equal measure: harvests, prices, the health of the powerful, war. The Лунникъ works from the day of the lunar month and the phase of the moon, forecasting weather, the fortune of a person born or falling ill on that day, and the days good and bad for particular undertakings; the Slavonic texts are pre-fifteenth-century and Greek in origin. The Мартолой, which the indices gloss as astrologer or astronomy, is a collection of astrological signs bearing on birth and illness.","Two of the genre are more idiosyncratic. The Трепетникъ predicts from involuntary movements of the body — a twitching eyelid, a jumping muscle, a ringing ear, an itching palm — proceeding through the body part by part, and it is the Slavonic representative of what the Greeks practised as palmomancy, a sub-discipline with a continuous ancient pedigree and Judaeo-Arabic parallels among the Genizah fragments. The Колядникъ takes the day of the week on which Christmas falls and derives from it the character of the whole coming year: the winter, the harvest, the sickness, the wars. Its logic is calendrical rather than astral, which may be why it proved so durable; the same scheme survives in western Europe in the various Revelaciones Esdrae. Alongside these the indices list a Рафли, generally understood as geomancy and whose name is connected with Arabic raml, sand-divination; a Зелейникъ, herbal; a Звездочетецъ, star-reader; an Остролог and a Зодий; and a Волховникъ and a Чаровникъ that no one has ever found.","The paradox of the genre is documentary rather than moral. These books were forbidden, repeatedly and specifically, and the prohibitions were reissued for six hundred years — which is itself evidence that they were ineffective. More than that: the manuscripts in which the indices of renounced books survive are frequently the same manuscripts in which the renounced books survive. A miscellany may open with a canonical text, present the list of what a Christian must not read, and then, a few gatherings later, offer a Trepetnikŭ or a set of thunder prognostics copied out in a careful liturgical hand. M. N. Speranskii built four monographs on this material between 1899 and 1908 and could hardly avoid noticing it. The most economical reading is not that the scribes were cynical but that the categories were less operative than the legislation implies: a book of thunder-signs was not, to the man copying it, in the same class as a heresy, and the index was a text to be transmitted like any other."],terms:[{native:"Сносудецъ",translit:"Snosudecŭ",gloss:"'dream-judger'; the index's name for the dreambook, a calque of Greek ὀνειροκριτικόν, and one of the earliest divinatory titles in Slavonic"},{native:"сънъ",translit:"sŭnŭ",gloss:"sleep, dream; the OCS word behind all the later names for the genre"},{native:"Сонникъ",translit:"Sonnikŭ",gloss:"the later and now universal Russian name for a dreambook, still in use for the popular printed article"},{native:"Громникъ",translit:"Gromnikŭ",gloss:"the thunder-book, from gromŭ, thunder; prognostication from the month or zodiacal sign in which thunder is first heard. Several redactions, all from a Greek brontologion"},{native:"Трепетникъ",translit:"Trepetnikŭ",gloss:"the twitch-book, from trepetŭ, a tremor; divination from involuntary movements of the body, the Slavonic form of Greek palmomancy"},{native:"Колядникъ",translit:"Koljadnikŭ",gloss:"prognostication for the year from the weekday on which Christmas falls; named from Koljada, the midwinter feast"},{native:"Лунникъ",translit:"Lunnikŭ",gloss:"the lunar book: weather, health, fortune and the fitness of days, reckoned from the day of the moon. Slavonic texts pre-date the fifteenth century and translate Greek originals"},{native:"Рафли",translit:"Rafli",gloss:"a divinatory book listed in the indices, generally understood as geomancy and connected with Arabic raml, sand-divination, though the route of transmission is unclear"}],texts:[{title:"The Slavonic Сносудецъ / Сонникъ",note:"The anonymous dreambook attributed to the prophet Daniel; related to the Latin Somniale Danielis and to the Byzantine dreambooks going under the name of Achmet, and ultimately to Artemidorus."},{title:"The Громникъ (Gromnikŭ)",note:"Known in several Slavonic redactions, all descending from a Greek brontologion; South Slavic translation no later than the fourteenth century, Russian copies from the fifteenth to the eighteenth. No translator is named."},{title:"The Трепетникъ (Trepetnikŭ)",note:"Body-part-by-body-part prognostication from twitches and itches; the Slavonic branch of ancient palmomancy, with Greek, Latin and Judaeo-Arabic cousins."},{title:"The Колядникъ (Koljadnikŭ)",note:"The year's character deduced from the weekday of Christmas; listed among the forbidden books and copied without interruption regardless."},{title:"M. N. Speranskii, Из истории отреченных книг, 4 parts (St Petersburg, 1899–1908)",note:"The foundational study of the Slavonic divinatory books — psalter divination, the Trepetniki, the Lopatochnik or shoulder-blade divination, and Aristotle's Gates — and of the manuscripts that carry them alongside the lists forbidding them."},{title:"W. F. Ryan, The Bathhouse at Midnight (1999), chapters on dreams and body divination",note:"The clearest English account of how each of these genres reached Slavonic, what its Greek ancestor was, and how the copies actually behave."}]},{slug:"astrological-calendrical-lore",title:"Astrological and Calendrical Lore",native:"Шестокрылъ",translit:"Šestokrylŭ",summary:"Six wings of eclipse tables out of Provence by way of Kiev, a heresy trial whose name was invented by its prosecutor, and a paschal calendar that ran out in the year seven thousand.",paragraphs:["Orthodox Slavdom inherited from Byzantium both a body of astronomical learning and a theological prohibition on using it to predict a person's fate, and the two arrived in the same crates. John the Exarch's Шестодневъ, the Bulgarian Hexaemeron of the early tenth century, discusses the heavens at length while denying that the stars govern; the Christian Topography of Cosmas Indicopleustes circulated in Slavonic with its flat, tabernacle-shaped cosmos; and the calendrical apparatus of the church — the reckoning of Easter, the nineteen-year lunar cycle, the twenty-eight-year solar cycle, the fifteen-year indiction — was itself an astronomical discipline that every literate cleric had to handle. Kirik of Novgorod's Учение о числах of 1136, the earliest Rus mathematical text, is a computistical work of exactly this kind. Judicial astrology, the casting of nativities, was the forbidden part, and it is the part for which the evidence in Slavonic before the fifteenth century is thin.","That changed in the 1470s with the Шестокрылъ. The Hebrew original, Shesh Kenafayim, was composed around 1350 by Immanuel Bonfils of Tarascon in Provence: six sets — the six wings — of astronomical tables for the movements of the sun and moon, permitting the calculation of new moons and of the times and magnitudes of solar and lunar eclipses. It reached Novgorod in the 1470s in a Slavonic version made in the Ruthenian lands, part of a small but genuinely remarkable body of fifteenth-century translations directly from Hebrew into Ruthenian in the Grand Duchy of Lithuania. Archbishop Gennadii of Novgorod, who led the campaign against the heresy of those years, complained specifically that the book's Jewish era made the world some seventeen hundred years younger than the Byzantine reckoning did — the two eras differ by 1,747 years — and that this was being used to argue the church had got its chronology wrong. The offence was not eclipse prediction as such. It was that a table could be made to embarrass the calendar.","The controversy of the 1470s to 1490s is conventionally called the heresy of the Judaizers, and the label needs handling with tongs: it is the coinage of the prosecution, above all of Iosif Volotskii, whose Просветитель is at once the fullest account of the group's alleged doctrines and an advocate's brief. The chronicle tradition says a certain Skhariya arrived at Novgorod in 1470 in the retinue of Prince Mikhail Olelkovich and began the corruption there; the movement is said to have spread to Moscow, to the court, and into the chancery. Councils condemned it in 1490 and again in 1504, and after the second several of the condemned were burned alive at Moscow and Novgorod. What the accused actually held is genuinely uncertain. Some modern scholars read a rationalising and anti-monastic current within the Russian church; some read a real engagement with Jewish learning, which the translations from Hebrew do attest; and some read a largely constructed heresy, for which the phrase phantom heresy has been used. Moshe Taube's work on the Kievan translations from Hebrew is the soberest evidence for what texts were in fact moving, as distinct from what the prosecutors said was believed.","The whole affair sat inside an eschatological deadline. The Byzantine world year began in 5508 before the Christian era, which made 1492 the year 7000 from creation, and the paschal tables in use had been computed only to that terminus. Whether the great mass of people expected the end in that year is disputed by historians, but the churchmen certainly had to decide what to do when the tables ran out. Metropolitan Zosima produced in 1492 an Изложение пасхалии, a presentation of the paschalion extending the reckoning into the eighth thousand years, and in the preface to it he called Ivan III the new Constantine of the new city of Constantine, Moscow — an early and now much-cited formulation of the imperial idea that Filofei of Pskov would give its famous shape in the next generation. The end of the world did not come; the eighth thousand years began; and the church emerged from the episode with a longer calendar and a new political theology, while the people condemned in 1504 did not emerge at all."],terms:[{native:"Шестокрылъ",translit:"Šestokrylŭ",gloss:"Six Wings; the Slavonic version of Immanuel Bonfils's Hebrew astronomical tables Shesh Kenafayim, composed at Tarascon around 1350 and condemned in Novgorod in the 1470s"},{native:"Шестодневъ",translit:"Šestodnevŭ",gloss:"Hexaemeron, the six days; John the Exarch of Bulgaria's cosmological work of the early tenth century, which transmits Greek astronomy while denying astral fate"},{native:"Пасхалия",translit:"Paschalija",gloss:"the paschalion, the tables for computing Easter; the ones in use expired with the year 7000 from creation, which fell in 1492"},{native:"Изложение пасхалии",translit:"Izloženie paschalii",gloss:"Metropolitan Zosima's Presentation of the Paschalion, 1492, which extended the tables into the eighth thousand years and in its preface called Moscow the new city of Constantine"},{native:"Звездочетецъ",translit:"Zvezdočetecŭ",gloss:"'star-reckoner'; a title on the indices of renounced books, one of several the lists give for astrological handbooks"},{native:"Острологъ",translit:"Ostrologŭ",gloss:"astrologer; another index title, whose underlying text — if there was a single one — has not been securely identified"},{native:"Мартолой",translit:"Martoloj",gloss:"a book the indices gloss as astrologer or astronomy: astrological signs bearing on a person's birth and illnesses"},{native:"Просветитель",translit:"Prosvetitelĭ",gloss:"The Enlightener, Iosif Volotskii's polemic against the Novgorod-Moscow heresy; the fullest account of the accused, written by their chief accuser"}],texts:[{title:"The Шестокрылъ (Šestokrylŭ)",note:"Six sets of lunar and solar tables for new moons and eclipses, from the Hebrew of Immanuel Bonfils of Tarascon, c. 1350; in Slavonic by the 1470s and attacked by Gennadii of Novgorod for the chronology implied by its Jewish era."},{title:"John the Exarch of Bulgaria, Шестодневъ (Hexaemeron)",note:"Early tenth century, Preslav: a compilation from Basil, Severian and others that carries a great deal of Greek cosmology into Slavonic while rejecting astral determinism."},{title:"Kirik of Novgorod, Учение о числах (1136)",note:"The earliest surviving mathematical and computistical treatise from Rus: cycles, indictions, the reckoning of time from creation. The respectable face of the same science."},{title:"Iosif Volotskii, Просветитель (The Enlightener)",note:"The principal source for the doctrines attributed to the Novgorod-Moscow accused of the 1470s–1500s, and an openly prosecutorial one. Its account should not be read as a neutral report, still less repeated in its own voice."},{title:"Metropolitan Zosima, Изложение пасхалии (1492)",note:"The new paschal tables for the eighth thousand years, with the preface naming Ivan III the new Constantine of Moscow — a foundational text for what became the Third Rome idea."},{title:"Moshe Taube, on the fifteenth-century Ruthenian translations from Hebrew and the heresy trials",note:"On what was actually translated from Hebrew in the Grand Duchy of Lithuania in the fifteenth century, and how loosely — or not — it connects to the accusations at Novgorod and Moscow."}]},{slug:"bogomil-and-dualist-traditions",title:"Bogomil and Dualist Traditions",native:"Сатанаилъ",translit:"Satanailŭ",summary:"A Bulgarian priest, an elder son said to have made the visible world, a single surviving text that may be their own, centuries of burning, and a modern national myth that has to be separated from all of it.",paragraphs:["Cosmas the Priest opens his Беседа, the Sermon Against the Heretics, by saying that in the land of Bulgaria, in the years of the Orthodox tsar Peter, there appeared a priest named Bogomil. Peter reigned from 927 to 969. An earlier and much briefer notice survives in a letter of Patriarch Theophylact of Constantinople to Peter about a newly appeared heresy, but Cosmas, generally placed in the middle or second half of the tenth century, is the earliest substantial source and by a wide margin the most detailed. He is also an advocate for the prosecution, writing to instruct clergy on how to answer heretics rather than to describe them, and his work reaches us only in later copies. The name Bogomil means dear to God; the polemical tradition enjoyed inverting it. Everything he reports has to be read as a hostile summary, which does not mean it is false — his account is coherent and it agrees at many points with the independent Greek reports. It does mean that the shape of the doctrine as we have it is the shape a critic gave it, and that no Bogomil catechism has ever been found against which to check him.","The doctrine, as the sources report it, begins before creation. Satanail is said to be God's elder son and steward, second in the heavens; his pride carries him and a portion of the angels out, and it is he, not God, who forms the visible world — earth, sun, moon, the bodies of animals, and the body of Adam, into which God alone breathes the soul. On this account matter is not merely fallen but authored by the adversary, and the human being is a soul of divine origin lodged in a fabric of enemy make. Christ is the younger son, sent to undo the elder; his body is appearance rather than flesh; and at his victory Satanail is said to lose the angelic suffix -il from his name and become simply Satan. From this the reported positions follow with some rigour. The Old Testament, being the record of the world-maker's dealings, is rejected or drastically curtailed. The cross is described as the gallows on which the Lord was murdered and no fit object of veneration. Icons, relics, church buildings, the material eucharist and water baptism are all said to be instruments made of Satanail's substance; the true baptism is reported to be the laying of the Gospel on the head with the Lord's Prayer. The hierarchy is rejected as well, and Cosmas reports that the Bogomils taught contempt for lords and for labour on their behalf — a detail which, if accurate, indicates why the Bulgarian state cared.","One text survives that is plausibly Bogomil rather than a report about Bogomils: the Interrogatio Iohannis, the Secret Supper, in which John at the Last Supper asks and Christ answers about the ordering of the world, the fall of the steward of the seventh heaven, the making of Adam, and the end. It is preserved in Latin only, in two witnesses — one at Vienna, one in the inquisitorial archive at Carcassonne. The Carcassonne copy carries an inquisitor's note that this is the secret book of the heretics of Concorezzo, brought from Bulgaria by their bishop Nazarius, and that it is full of error; the transfer is usually placed around 1190. No Slavonic original survives, which is the central difficulty: we possess what may be the Bogomils' own voice only in the translation their enemies kept, in a copy their enemies made in order to condemn it. Edina Bozóky's edition of 1980 and the translations in Janet and Bernard Hamilton's Christian Dualist Heresies in the Byzantine World (1998) are where the text can be read with its problems attached.","The persecution is as much a part of the history as the doctrine, and more securely documented. In or about 1111 a Bogomil teacher named Basil, a physician, was drawn into a false confidence by the emperor Alexios I, who had the conversation taken down by a secretary behind a curtain, and was burned in the Hippodrome; Anna Komnene tells the story in the fifteenth book of the Alexiad with evident satisfaction, and Euthymius Zigabenus set out the doctrines in the twenty-seventh title of his Panoplia Dogmatica on the strength of the interrogations. In Bulgaria, Tsar Boril convened a synod at Tarnovo in 1211 whose anathemas, preserved in the Slavonic Synodikon that bears his name, have no Greek counterpart and were composed for the occasion. In Bosnia, representatives of the local church abjured at Bilino Polje in 1203 before a papal legate. Dualists were hunted in Byzantium, in Bulgaria, in Serbia under Stefan Nemanja, and later, as Cathars, across Languedoc and Lombardy. Whatever one makes of the theology, the people who held it were interrogated under compulsion, exiled and killed over five centuries, and the tone of the sources that describe them should not be adopted as one's own.","Finally, the Bosnian question, which has to be handled carefully because it is not only a historical matter. The medieval Crkva bosanska, the Bosnian Church, had its own hierarchy under an officer called the djed and its members called themselves krstjani, Christians — the word is cited from the charters in the Latin transcription customary in the scholarship, since the medieval Bosnian Cyrillic forms vary and the modern Serbian letter ј in which it is often printed is a nineteenth-century creation. From the later nineteenth century — Franjo Rački above all, with Arthur Evans and János Asbóth carrying the idea further — this church was identified with Bogomilism, and the great carved tombstones of Bosnia and Hum, the stećci, were read as Bogomil monuments; the picture was then absorbed into competing national narratives, including a story that the Bogomils converted en masse to Islam after 1463 out of affinity or resentment. Scholarship has dismantled nearly all of it, and the identification is now contested at best and by most specialists rejected. John Fine's The Bosnian Church (1975) found nothing in the surviving Bosnian evidence that is demonstrably dualist; Dubravko Lovrenović and others showed that the stećci were raised over Catholics, Orthodox and members of the Bosnian Church alike, and that their inter-confessional character is one of their defining features; the mass-conversion story has no contemporary support. What remains true, and is a different kind of truth, is that the memory of a persecuted native church is part of how the region narrates itself, and that this memory is doing work in the present whether or not its medieval premises hold."],terms:[{native:"Богомилъ",translit:"Bogomilŭ",gloss:"'dear to God'; the name Cosmas gives to the priest in whom he says the movement began, and thereafter the name of the movement itself"},{native:"Сатанаилъ",translit:"Satanailŭ",gloss:"Satanail, reported as the elder son and maker of the visible world; the -il is the angelic theophoric ending, and in the doctrine ascribed to the Bogomils he loses it at his defeat and becomes simply Satana"},{native:"Беседа",translit:"Beseda",gloss:"discourse, sermon; the customary short title of Cosmas the Priest's Sermon Against the Heretics, the earliest full source for Bogomil doctrine, openly hostile, and preserved only in later copies"},{native:"крьстъ",translit:"krĭstŭ",gloss:"cross; rejected by the Bogomils as their opponents report, on the ground that one does not venerate the instrument on which a man was killed"},{native:"Синодикъ",translit:"Sinodikŭ",gloss:"the Synodikon; the Bulgarian recension made for Tsar Boril in 1211 carries anti-Bogomil anathemas that exist in no Greek exemplar"},{native:"крьстиꙗнинъ",translit:"krĭstijaninŭ",gloss:"Christian; the Old Church Slavonic form given here. The members of the medieval Bosnian Church called themselves krstjani, a word conventionally cited from the charters in Latin transcription rather than in a modernised Cyrillic spelling"},{native:"дѣдъ",translit:"dědŭ",gloss:"'grandfather'; the title of the head of the Bosnian Church. Its holders' beliefs are not documented in any surviving text of their own"},{native:"тайная вечеря",translit:"tajnaja večerja",gloss:"the secret supper; the modern Slavic rendering of the Latin title of the Interrogatio Iohannis. No Slavonic text of the work survives, and no medieval Slavonic title for it is attested"}],texts:[{title:"Cosmas the Priest, Беседа против еретиков (Sermon Against the Heretics)",note:"Tenth century, most probably after 969; the earliest full source for Bogomilism and a work of prosecution, surviving only in later copies. Translated in Janet and Bernard Hamilton, Christian Dualist Heresies in the Byzantine World c.650–c.1450 (Manchester, 1998)."},{title:"Interrogatio Iohannis (the Book of the Secret Supper)",note:"The only text that may be Bogomil rather than about Bogomils; survives in Latin alone, at Vienna and at Carcassonne. The Carcassonne colophon credits Nazarius, Cathar bishop of Concorezzo, with bringing it from Bulgaria. Edition by Edina Bozóky (1980)."},{title:"Euthymius Zigabenus, Panoplia Dogmatica, title 27",note:"The fullest Greek exposition of Bogomil doctrine, compiled at the court of Alexios I on the basis of interrogations, including Basil's — that is, on the basis of testimony extracted under threat."},{title:"Anna Komnene, Alexiad, book XV",note:"The account of Basil the physician, the confession recorded behind a curtain, and the burning in the Hippodrome in or about 1111."},{title:"The Synodikon of Tsar Boril (1211)",note:"The Slavonic Synodikon of Orthodoxy in its Bulgarian recension, made for the synod at Tarnovo; its anti-Bogomil anathemas were composed in Bulgaria and have no Greek original."},{title:"John V. A. Fine, The Bosnian Church: A New Interpretation (1975; reissued 2007)",note:"The study that removed the scholarly foundation from the identification of the Bosnian Church with Bogomilism, and with it from a century of national myth-making about the stećci."}]},{slug:"books-of-secrets-and-forbidden-lists",title:"The Books of Secrets and the Forbidden Lists",native:"отреченныя книги",translit:"otrečennyja knigi",summary:"Six centuries of lists of what must not be read — and the discovery that for a good many titles the prohibition is the only trace the book ever left.",paragraphs:["The Slavonic index of renounced books begins as a translation. Byzantium had inherited from the fourth century onward a series of lists distinguishing the canonical from the disputed and the spurious — Athanasius's thirty-ninth festal letter, the Stichometry of Nicephorus, the catalogue that circulates under the name of Anastasius of Sinai — and when these were rendered into Slavonic they came with the rest of the patristic furniture. The earliest surviving Slavonic example is in the Изборникъ of 1073, the Miscellany of Svjatoslav, copied at Kiev from a Bulgarian exemplar made for Tsar Symeon: an encyclopaedic compilation of patristic excerpts, tropes and figures, grammar, logic, riddles, and a list of forbidden titles of which only a handful were in fact available in Slavonic at that date. A further list travelled with the works of Nikon of the Black Mountain, composed in the later eleventh century and translated into Slavonic afterwards. At this stage the index is a piece of imported apparatus, describing a Greek book-world rather than a Slavonic one.","It did not stay that way. Over the following five centuries the lists grew, and what they grew by is local. Fourteenth-century South Slavic and Russian copies add the fables of Solomon and Kitovras, the Bogomil books, and above all the divinatory handbooks: the Громникъ, the Колядникъ, the Зелейникъ, the Волховникъ, the Рафли, the Остролог, the Зодий, the Звездочетецъ, a Розгомечецъ and a Чаровникъ, among others. The Stoglav council of 1551 gathered the prohibitions and reissued them with the force of a national canon-law code. By the seventeenth century the list has become a genre with its own recensions, its own textual history and its own scribal errors, and one of the standing problems is that some entries look like corruptions of others, so that the number of distinct books being forbidden is not necessarily the number of names on the page. The nineteenth-century editions of A. N. Pypin (1862) and N. S. Tikhonravov (1863) printed the lists and much of what they forbade; Speranskii then spent a career on the relation between the two.","Here is the perversity that gives this current its title. For a considerable number of the entries, the index is the only evidence that the book existed. The Волховникъ — the sorcerer's book — is named repeatedly and has never been found; so is the Чаровникъ, described in one list as consisting of twelve chapters on transformation into animals, a description that is our entire knowledge of it. These are books known only from their condemnation, and must be described that way. Whether they were real books that have perished, or half-remembered titles, or in some cases inventions of the list-makers copying one another, is not decidable from the evidence we have. The result is a bibliography of absences. The church, in trying to close a library, catalogued it, and its catalogue is now the finding aid for a literature it destroyed or never quite located.","The greatest of the condemned vernacular cosmologies is not strictly a book at all. The Голубиная книга is a Russian spiritual verse, a духовный стих, anonymous and known in some twenty recorded versions ranging from thirty lines to more than nine hundred; the versions we have are overwhelmingly nineteenth-century transcriptions from singers, and although the poem is generally held to be considerably older, no date of composition can be fixed and the proposals should be treated as proposals. A great book falls from a cloud — too large to be carried, too heavy to be read — and a king, usually David, answers from memory the questions that a second king, often called Volot Volotovich, puts to him. Which city is mother of cities, which sea of seas, which stone of stones, which bird of birds, which beast of beasts; and, before all that, where the sun and the moon and the stars and the winds came from, which the poem answers by deriving them from the body and the garments of God. The name itself is contested: V. N. Mochulskii held that Голубиная, of the dove, is a reworking of Глубинная, of the deep, and V. N. Toporov proposed comparison with Iranian cosmological material. The church banned it as a mixture of apocrypha and paganism, and it was still being sung to collectors in the nineteenth century, by which time the Russian symbolists had begun to hear something else in it.","The paradox is worth stating plainly rather than cleverly. Censorship in this tradition was not efficient enough to suppress and not indifferent enough to ignore; what it produced instead was a continuous, itemised, six-hundred-year record of what people in Bulgaria, Serbia and Rus were actually reading outside the canon. Scholars of Slavonic apocrypha use it as a bibliography. Scholars of divination use it as a checklist against which to identify anonymous manuscripts. And the discomfort at the heart of it — that we know this literature best through the eyes of the men who wanted it gone — is one that the reader of any of these currents should keep in view, because it applies to the charms, to the dualists and to the dreambooks alike."],terms:[{native:"отреченныя книги",translit:"otrečennyja knigi",gloss:"the renounced books; the standing Church Slavonic name for the index's contents, from otrešti sę, to renounce"},{native:"ложныя книги",translit:"ložnyja knigi",gloss:"false books; the other standing term, and the one that pairs with istinnyja, true, in the double lists of what may and may not be read"},{native:"истинныя книги",translit:"istinnyja knigi",gloss:"the true books; the positive half of the index, whose existence is a reminder that the genre began as a canon list rather than as a prohibition"},{native:"Изборникъ",translit:"Izbornikŭ",gloss:"miscellany, selection; the Izbornik of 1073, copied at Kiev from a Bulgarian exemplar, carries the earliest surviving Slavonic index of forbidden titles"},{native:"Стоглавъ",translit:"Stoglavŭ",gloss:"the Hundred Chapters, the acts of the Moscow council of 1551, which restated the prohibitions and gave them the force of a national code"},{native:"Волховникъ",translit:"Volchovnikŭ",gloss:"the sorcerer's book; named repeatedly on the indices and never once found. Known only from its condemnation, like several other titles on the lists"},{native:"Голубиная книга",translit:"Golubinaja kniga",gloss:"the Book of the Dove — or of the Deep, since Golubinaja has been argued to be a reshaping of Glubinnaja; the great Russian cosmological spiritual verse, anonymous throughout"},{native:"духовный стих",translit:"duchovnyj stich",gloss:"spiritual verse; the sung vernacular religious poetry, largely the province of blind itinerant singers, in which the Book of the Dove survives"}],texts:[{title:"The Изборникъ of 1073 (Miscellany of Svjatoslav)",note:"Copied at Kiev from a Bulgarian exemplar made for Tsar Symeon; among much else it carries the earliest surviving Slavonic index of forbidden books, most of whose titles were not yet current in Slavonic."},{title:"The indices of renounced books, fourteenth to seventeenth century",note:"A growing family of anonymous lists, increasingly local in content, naming the divinatory handbooks, the Solomon and Kitovras fables, and the dualist literature. Their recensional history is itself a field of study."},{title:"The Stoglav (1551)",note:"The hundred chapters of the Moscow council: prohibitions on sorcerers, diviners and renounced books, restated with legal force and, incidentally, itemised."},{title:"A. N. Pypin, Ложные и отреченные книги русской старины (1862)",note:"Volume 3 of the Памятники старинной русской литературы; the first large printed corpus of the forbidden literature itself."},{title:"N. S. Tikhonravov, Памятники отреченной русской литературы, 2 vols (1863)",note:"The companion corpus, and with Pypin the foundation on which all later study of the Slavonic apocrypha and divinatory books rests."},{title:"The Голубиная книга (Book of the Dove)",note:"An anonymous Russian spiritual verse in some twenty recorded versions, thirty to over nine hundred lines, most of them taken down from singers in the nineteenth century. Condemned by the church, and sung regardless."}]},{slug:"greek-and-byzantine-occult-works-in-slavic-dress",title:"Greek and Byzantine Occult Works in Slavic Dress",native:"Тайная тайных",translit:"Tajnaja tajnych",summary:"The translation channel itself: a beast-book, a romance of Alexander, a mirror for princes that reached Muscovy through Hebrew rather than Latin, and the occasions on which a Slavonic word preserves a Greek one now lost.",paragraphs:["Almost everything in this hall came through translation, and the character of the translations is part of the evidence. The first wave is Cyrillo-Methodian and then Preslavian and Ohridian, ninth and tenth centuries, and it is deliberately literal: Slavonic was being built as a calquing language, with compounds assembled part by part on the Greek model, and the result is that a Slavonic text can often be run backwards to something close to its exemplar's wording. The second great wave is the fourteenth century, associated with Euthymius of Tarnovo in Bulgaria, with the Serbian houses and with Athos, and it is more literal still, deliberately archaising, and produced under a hesychast concern for exactness of terminology. Isaija of Serres's Dionysius of 1371 belongs to it. This is the technical reason why Slavonic matters to the textual criticism of Greek works: not because Slavic scribes were more careful than Greek ones, but because their translation practice was tight enough that their choices are diagnostic.","The Физиологъ is the classic case of transmission. The Greek Physiologus, second-century Alexandrian in its oldest recension, describes some fifty animals, birds and stones and reads each as a moral or Christological sign — the pelican reviving its young, the phoenix, the panther whose breath is sweet, the unicorn caught only by a virgin. An Old Bulgarian translation of the oldest recension was made at Preslav around the turn of the ninth and tenth centuries. It did not survive in Bulgaria at all: it is known only from late Russian copies of the fifteenth to seventeenth centuries, which is an exact illustration of this hall's general condition. Ana Stoykova has shown that two distinct Slavonic versions circulated. That the church tolerated the Physiologus while forbidding the Trepetnikŭ is instructive about where the line ran — the beast-book teaches a doctrine through nature, whereas the twitch-book claims to read the future out of it.","The Александрия, the Slavonic Alexander Romance, descends from Pseudo-Callisthenes and arrived twice: a Bulgarian translation of the tenth or eleventh century, and a Serbian version of the fourteenth century which became by far the more popular. It brought with it the marvels of the East, the beast-catalogue that gave Slavonic translators such trouble, the letters between Alexander and Aristotle and Dindimus, and the episodes that made the Romance a natural home for the occult imagination and that recur, with variation, across its recensions: the speaking trees of the sun and the moon, the journey to the gates of paradise, the flight aloft and the descent into the sea. It was absorbed into the Russian chronographs, so that a reader encountering it there met it as history. The Пчела, the Slavonic Melissa, brought classical gnomic wisdom by the same road, and the Луцидариусъ, the Elucidarium, brought a western cosmology into sixteenth-century Muscovy from German — a reminder that the channel was not always Greek.","The most remarkable of these transmissions is the Тайная тайных, the Slavonic Secret of Secrets. The work behind it is the Arabic Sirr al-asrār, a vast mirror for princes attributed to Aristotle and addressed to Alexander — an attribution nobody now defends — containing statecraft, regimen, physiognomy, materia medica, poisons and talismanic and onomantic matter. In Latin Christendom it was one of the most copied books of the Middle Ages, and everywhere else in Europe it came through the Latin. In Slavonic it did not. The short Arabic recension was translated into Hebrew in the thirteenth century, and from that Hebrew a Ruthenian translation was made at Kiev in the second half of the fifteenth century, in the Grand Duchy of Lithuania, with copies passing into Muscovy afterwards. W. F. Ryan and Moshe Taube's edition (Warburg Institute, 2019) sets out the case; the translator has been identified with a Kievan Jew, and the further identification of that man with the Zechariah or Skhariya named in the Novgorod heresy accusations has been proposed but remains uncertain. The Аристотелевы врата, Aristotle's Gates, which the indices forbid, is generally taken to be this book.","As for a Slavonic translator preserving a Greek reading now lost: the strongest instances are whole works rather than variants — the Apocalypse of Abraham and the Ladder of Jacob, which exist in no other language, and 2 Enoch, for which the Slavonic carries the whole continuous text. But the phenomenon operates at the level of the word as well. Where a translator has calqued a Greek compound element by element, the Slavonic can show which of two attested Greek readings stood in his exemplar; where he has transliterated rather than translated a technical term, the transliteration can preserve a form the Greek manuscripts have normalised away; and the Slavonic Physiologus, standing on the oldest recension, occasionally supports a reading against the later Greek families. The caution is the same one that governs the whole hall. Slavonic evidence is usable only where the translator's technique has been established for that text, by that scribe, in that recension. Where it has not been, a Slavonic reading is a hypothesis about a Greek reading, and the honest editions say so on the page."],terms:[{native:"Тайная тайных",translit:"Tajnaja tajnych",gloss:"the Secret of Secrets; the Slavonic Secretum secretorum, translated at Kiev in the later fifteenth century from a Hebrew version of the Arabic, not from the Latin as everywhere else in Europe"},{native:"Аристотелевы врата",translit:"Aristotelevy vrata",gloss:"Aristotle's Gates; the title under which the indices forbid a book generally identified with the Slavonic Secret of Secrets"},{native:"Физиологъ",translit:"Fiziologŭ",gloss:"the Physiologus; translated at Preslav from the oldest Greek recension, lost in Bulgaria, and surviving only in Russian copies of the fifteenth to seventeenth centuries"},{native:"Александрия",translit:"Aleksandrija",gloss:"the Alexander Romance from Pseudo-Callisthenes; a Bulgarian translation of the tenth or eleventh century and a Serbian one of the fourteenth, the latter much the more widely copied"},{native:"Пчела",translit:"Pčela",gloss:"'the Bee'; the Slavonic Melissa, a florilegium of classical and patristic sayings arranged by topic, and the main route by which pagan gnomic wisdom entered Slavonic"},{native:"Луцидариусъ",translit:"Lucidariusŭ",gloss:"the Elucidarium in Slavonic dress, reaching Muscovy in the sixteenth century from German — evidence that by then the channel ran west as well as south"},{native:"прѣводъ",translit:"prěvodŭ",gloss:"translation, rendering; the OCS term, and by extension the name for a particular recension of a translated text"},{native:"кънигꙑ",translit:"kŭnigy",gloss:"books, writings; the Old Church Slavonic plural, written with the yeru ꙑ of the Cyrillic extensions. The word covers a letter, a charter and a codex alike, which is why an index can forbid a book that was never more than a few leaves"}],texts:[{title:"The Тайная тайных (Secret of Secrets), Ruthenian, later fifteenth century",note:"From the Hebrew version of the short Arabic recension, made at Kiev; statecraft, regimen, physiognomy, poisons and talismanic material, pseudonymously ascribed to Aristotle. Edition, translation and index by W. F. Ryan and Moshe Taube, The Secret of Secrets: The East Slavic Version (Warburg Institute, 2019)."},{title:"The Slavonic Физиологъ (Physiologus)",note:"Anonymous Preslav translation of the oldest Alexandrian recension, c. 900; not preserved in Bulgaria, known only through Russian copies of the fifteenth to seventeenth centuries. Two Slavonic versions distinguished by Ana Stoykova."},{title:"The Александрия (Alexander Romance) in Slavonic",note:"From Pseudo-Callisthenes by way of the Byzantine recensions: a Bulgarian translation of the tenth or eleventh century, a Serbian one of the fourteenth, and absorption into the Russian chronographs."},{title:"The Corpus Areopagiticum Slavicum",note:"Isaija of Serres's complete Dionysius of 1371, with the scholia; the fourteenth-century translation technique that makes it a witness of interest for the Greek text as well as a monument of Serbian letters."},{title:"The Пчела (Melissa)",note:"A topically arranged florilegium of sayings from scripture, the Fathers and the classical authors; the principal Slavonic conduit for pagan moral philosophy, and untroubled by any index."},{title:"Moshe Taube, 'Transmission of Scientific Texts in 15th-Century Eastern Knaan', Aleph 10:2 (2010)",note:"On the Hebrew-to-Slavonic translation work in the Grand Duchy of Lithuania: what was rendered, by whom, and what the enterprise was for."}]}]};export{e as default};
